--TEST--
`Core\Serialize::decode` refuses bytes that name a generator's class, a function value's class or an open file's class, and it refuses each one before it compares a property — `rule:classes/serialize-is-a-closed-format`
--FILE--
<?nvs
class Counter
{
    public static function upTo(int $n): Iterator<int>
    {
        yield $n;
    }
}

class Maker
{
    public function adder(): callable
    {
        return fn(int $x): int => $x + 1;
    }
}

class Forge
{
    // Returns one byte with the value `$value`.
    public static function byte(uint $value): bytes
    {
        return Core\Bytes::fill(1, $value);
    }

    // Returns a payload with one object of class `$class` and 0 properties.
    // The bytes are `NVS`, 27, the version 1, the object node 9, the name and 0.
    public static function naming(string $class): bytes
    {
        bytes $name = $class as bytes;
        return Core\Bytes::join([
            'NVS' as bytes,
            Forge::byte(27),
            Forge::byte(1),
            Forge::byte(9),
            Forge::byte(Core\Bytes::length($name)),
            $name,
            Forge::byte(0),
        ]);
    }

    public static function decode(string $class): void
    {
        bytes $payload = Core\Taint::assertTrustedBytes(Forge::naming($class), 'this program wrote the bytes');
        try {
            Core\Serialize::decode($payload);
            echo "decoded\n";
        } catch (ParseError $e) {
            echo $e->message, "\n";
        }
    }
}

// The classes of a generator, of a function value and of an open file.
// Each class has more than 0 properties, but the error is about the class.
Forge::decode('Counter::upTo$gen');
Forge::decode('Maker$fn0');
Forge::decode('Core\IO\File');
--EXPECT--
Core\Serialize::decode(): a generator is a suspended frame, so it has no meaning on the other side of a copy boundary
Core\Serialize::decode(): a callable captures variables from the code that created it, so it has no meaning on the other side of a copy boundary
Core\Serialize::decode(): a Core\IO\File holds a handle onto something this side of the boundary opened, so it has no meaning on the other side of a copy boundary — pass what identifies the resource and open it there
